## 1. Introduction
In chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer, accumulation of extracellular matrix (ECM) proteins, leading to pancreatic fibrosis, is a constant pathological finding and considered as an aggravating factor of the tumour disease [1–3]. So far, there are no specific therapies available to prevent, retard or even reverse progressive fibrogenesis. PSC are the main source of ECM in the diseased organ [3–6]. In response to profibrogenic mediators, such as cytokines, growth factors, oxidant stress and ethanol metabolites, the cells undergo phenotypic changes termed “activation”. The activation process is associated with cell proliferation, expression of myofibroblastic markers, and increased synthesis of ECM proteins [4,5]. While many previous studies have focussed on mediators of stellate cell activation, e. g., transforming growth factor- $^{-\beta1}$ and the mitogen platelet-derived growth factor [7,8], antagonists of the activation process are less well characterised.
In two previous studies, we have analysed biological and molecular effects of interferons [9,10]. Our data revealed that these cytokines, especially $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ exert antifibrotic effects in PSC. Thus, $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ efficiently inhibited cell proliferation and collagen synthesis [9], and suppressed expression of connective tissue growth factor, an autocrine enhancer of ECM synthesis in PSC [10]. Several lines of evidence pointed to a key role of the transcription factor STAT1 in the mediation of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ effects in PSC: First, biological efficiency of different interferons closely correlated with their capability to activate STAT1. Secondly, downregulation of STAT1 expression by siRNA was associated with a diminished antiproliferative effect of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma.$ Thirdly, STAT1 overexpression under the control of a tetracycline-dependent promoter revealed a direct link between STAT1 expression and activation on one hand, and $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ -induced growth inhibition, apoptosis, and target gene expression on the other hand.
STAT1 is established as an essential mediator of signals generated by activated type I and II interferon receptors. In mice, STAT1 deficiency results in a compromised innate immunity and a high sensitivity to infection by microbial pathogens and viruses [11,12]. Activation of STAT1 is mediated by tyrosine kinases of the Janus kinase (JAK) family [13]. Thus, $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ binding to the type II receptor triggers activation of the receptor-associated kinases JAK1 and 2 [14]. JAKs phosphorylate tyrosine residues in the intracytoplasmic receptor chains, thereby creating docking sites for SH2-containing proteins including STAT1. Upon phosphorylation by $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ -activated JAKs, STAT1 forms homodimers that translocate to the nucleus, where they bind to $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ activation sites (GAS elements) in the promoter of target genes. Under physiological conditions, IFN signalling through STAT1 is tightly controlled at multiple levels. Key mechanisms of negative regulation include (1) STAT1 dephosphorylation by tyrosine phosphatases [15–17], (2) feedback inhibition of Janus kinases by suppressor of cytokine signalling (SOCS) proteins; in particular SOCS1 [18,19], (3) inhibition of STAT1 DNA binding and transcriptional activity by protein inhibitor of activated STAT (PIAS) proteins [20], and (4) diminished expression of signalling cascade components, such as the $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ receptor $\upbeta$ chain [21].
Given the key role of STAT1 in the mediation of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ effects, a detailed understanding of regulatory mechanisms controlling STAT1 activity in PSC may contribute to the development of a targeted antifibrotic therapy. Here, we have focussed on determinants of STAT1 pathway activity in the cells. As target genes of STAT1 signalling, SOCS1 [18,19] and interferon regulatory factor 1 (IRF1 [22]) were considered. In the course of the studies, a mathematical model was developed and tested. The model correctly reflected activation profiles of components of the STAT1 pathway, and successfully predicted the outcome of further experiments. Taken together, the results indicate that the contribution of SOCS1 to the termination of STAT1 activation in PSC is limited. We suggest exhaustion of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ and dephosphorylation of STAT1 by tyrosine phosphatases as critical steps of STAT1 inactivation in our experimental system.
## 2 . Materials and methods
## 2.1. Material
Iscove's modified Dulbecco's medium (IMDM) and supplements for cell culture were obtained from Biochrom (Berlin, Germany), recombinant rat $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ from Immunotools (Friesoythe, Germany), and bovine serum albumin (BSA) from Roche Diagnostics (Mannheim, Germany). TriReagent®, for RNA isolation, was purchased from Ambion (Austin, TX, USA). Reverse transcription and Taqman™ reagents were delivered by Applied Biosystems (Foster City, CA, USA). Primary antibodies were from BD Biosciences (Franklin Lakes, NJ, USA; anti-pY701–STAT1; mouse monoclonal), Upstate Cell Signalling Solutions (Billerica, MA, USA; anti-ERK 1/2; rabbit polyclonal) and Santa Cruz (Santa Cruz, CA, USA; anti-STAT1; rabbit polyclonal). Fluorescently-labelled secondary antibodies for immunoblot analysis and Odyssey® blocking buffer were delivered by LICOR (Lincoln, NE, USA). Goat anti-rabbit Alexa Fluor® 488 IgG for immunofluorescence studies was purchased from MobiTec (Göttingen, Germany), polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) membrane from Millipore (Billerica, MA, USA), and Draq5™ from Biostatus Limited (Leicestershire, UK). Prestained Protein Marker was supplied by New England Biolabs (Frankfurt, Germany). Standard laboratory chemicals were from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO, USA).
## 2.2. Cell culture and IFNγ treatment
Immortalised PSC [23] were cultured in IMDM supplemented with $10\%$ heat-inactivated fetal calf serum, $1\%$ non-essential amino acids (dilution of a $100\mathrm{X}$ stock solution), $100\mathrm{U/ml}$ penicillin and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ streptomycin at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ humidified atmosphere.
In single stimulation experiments, the cells were treated with the indicated amount of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ for different periods of time. In multistimulation studies, the overall dose $(1~\mathrm{ng/ml})$ of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ was either applied in a single stimulation mode, or split into four doses of $0.25\mathrm{ng/ml}$ as described below. Repeated stimulations were performed by adding the appropriate amount of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ to the already present culture medium. Hence, cultures of the dose-split series were treated with an overall lower dose of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ than cultures of single stimulation experiments, if they were harvested during the first $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ after initiation of stimulation.
## 2.3. Immunoblotting
Cells were harvested by medium aspiration and addition of boiling lysis buffer $2\%$ sodium dodecyl sulphate [SDS], $10\%$ glycerol, $5\mathrm{mM}$ ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid $\mathrm{[pH8.0]}$ , $62.5\mathrm{mM}$ Tris–HCl $\mathrm{[pH6},8\over$ , $0.01\%3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetrabromphenolsulfonphthalein, $5\%\upbeta$ -mercaptoethanol) directly to the cell monolayer. Cellular proteins received from equal numbers of cells were separated by $8\%$ SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis and transferred onto PVDF membrane by tank blotting. Afterwards, membranes were blocked for 1 h using Odyssey® blocking buffer, before primary antibodies were added and incubation continued for another hour. Simultaneous detection of tyrosinephosphorylated STAT1 and total STAT1 was performed according to the instructions of LI-COR (Odyssey® protocol for two-colour immunoblotting). Antibody to ERK 1/2 was applied separately to the lower part of the membrane.
The following secondary antibodies were used for the simultaneous analysis of phospho-STAT1 and total STAT1: IRDye® 800CW conjugated goat anti-mouse IgG, and IRDye® 680 conjugated goat anti-rabbit IgG. The separate detection of ERK 1/2 was accomplished applying IRDye® 800CW conjugated goat anti-rabbit IgG. After 1 h of incubation, further analysis was performed using an Odyssey® Infrared Imaging System. All immunoblots were scanned at a wavelength of $700\mathrm{nm}$ for detecting IRDye® 680 labelled antibodies and at a wavelength of ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ for IRDye® 800CW conjugated antibodies. Signal intensities were quantified by means of the Odyssey® software version 3.0. Signals obtained for phosphorylated STAT1 and total STAT1 protein were normalised for loading differences by calculating the phospho-STAT1 (or STAT1) to ERK $1/2$ ratio. Under the experimental conditions used in this study, protein levels of ERK $1/2$ in PSC remained constant (data not shown).
## 2.4. Immunostaining and confocal microscopy
Immortalised PSC were cultured on glass coverslips until reaching approximately $20\%$ confluence. After stimulation with $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ as indicated, the cells were fixed for $10\mathrm{min}$ in ice-cold methanol $(70\%)$ . Blocking of nonspecific signals was performed by incubation with $1\%$ BSA (in PBS) for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature (RT). STAT1 protein was detected by incubation with specific antibody ( $30\mathrm{min}$ , RT), followed by two washing steps with PBS and application of Alexa Fluor® 488 goat antirabbit IgG (30 min, RT). Subsequently, the nuclei were counterstained with Draq5™ at a final concentration of $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (in PBS) for 5 min at RT. Finally, glass coverslips were embedded on microscope slides using mounting medium $25\%$ glycerol, $10\%$ polyvinyl alcohol, $0.4\%$ phenol, $0.05\mathrm{M}$ Tris, $\mathsf{p H}8.5$ ) and stored in the dark.
Samples were investigated using a confocal laser scanning microscope (Leica TCS SP2 AOBS). Images were further analysed using the Open Source software package ImageJ. To quantify nuclear translocation of STAT1, averaged ratios of nuclear versus cytoplasmic concentration of STAT1 (sum of pixel intensities, divided by area) were calculated. Therefore, 50 to 130 cells from three to four different images were analysed for each time point.
## 2.5. Quantitative reverse transcriptase-PCR using real-time TaqMan™ technology
## 2.6. Statistical analysis
Results are expressed as means $\pm$ standard error of the mean (SEM) for the indicated number of independent cultures per experimental protocol.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Experimental analysis of STAT1 activation and expression of STAT1 target genes
In initial studies, immortalised rat PSC were exposed to $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ at $100~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}$ ; a saturating dose with respect to the inhibition of proliferation and collagen synthesis [9]. We chose a narrow time course of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ stimulation for up to $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , and analysed tyrosine phosphorylation of STAT1 as well as STAT1 protein expression by means of Odyssey® infrared imaging technology (Fig. 1). Antibodies against phospho-STAT1 (upper panel) and STAT1 (middle panel) detected two protein bands at 91 and $84\mathrm{kDa}$ , corresponding to STAT1α and STAT1β isoforms. At all time points, the two STAT1 variants displayed concordant changes both at the level of phosphorylation and protein expression, and were therefore quantified together. The results of a quantitative analysis of STAT1 protein expression and phosphorylation are shown later on in this chapter in the context of mathematical modelling (Fig. 4A). They indicate a triphasic pattern of STAT1 tyrosine phosphorylation over the investigation period of $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ : A strong and rapid initial increase was followed by a plateau lasting approximately until $200\mathrm{{min}}$ after $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ application. Afterwards, a small but progressive further increase of phospho-STAT1 levels was observed. Until the end of the experiment, phospho-STAT1 levels did not decline. STAT1 protein levels remained quite constant during the first $200\mathrm{{min}}$ of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ treatment. Afterwards, however, a continuous increase was observed, resulting in more than 5-fold increased STAT1 protein levels near the end of the investigation period. This effect is likely to be the result of transcriptional induction because a parallel increase was detected at the level of STAT1 mRNA (data not shown).
In addition to tyrosine phosphorylation, cellular localisation of STAT1 was monitored by laser scanning microscopy. As shown in Fig. 2, short-term treatment for $20\mathrm{min}$ with $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ at $100\mathrm{ng/ml}$ induced nuclear translocation of STAT1, consistent with its activation by phosphorylation. $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ after $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ application, enhanced nuclear staining of STAT1 still persisted. Furthermore, an increased overall intensity of the signal, corresponding to the higher expression of STAT1 protein, was observed. Subsequently, a quantitative analysis of STAT1 staining intensities at various time points (up to $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) after cytokine application was performed. The results are embedded into Section 3.5 (Fig. 4A). They indicate an initial rapid increase of the ratio nuclear/cytoplasmic STAT1, which is followed by a decline nearly to the baseline at times $\geq400\mathrm{min}$ ; reflecting that both the levels of nuclear and cytoplasmic STAT1 are increased.
To study effects of STAT1 activation on target gene transcription, expression of SOCS1 was monitored by real-time PCR and compared to the one of IRF1, which is also expressed in a STAT1-dependent manner but does not execute feedback inhibitor functions [18,22]. In accordance with previous studies [24], the very labile SOCS1 protein was undetectable by immunoblot analysis (data not shown). The results (Fig. 4A) revealed that SOCS1 levels were low during the first $50\mathrm{min}$ of IFN treatment. Afterwards, a continuous increase to approximately 7-fold higher levels than in control cells was detected. Together, these data indicate that phospho-STAT1 levels remained high, despite induction of SOCS1 expression, for at least $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ after application of a high dose of $\mathsf{I F N\gamma}$ . On the other hand, the further increase of phospho-STAT1 at times $\geq200$ min after $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ application was small compared to the one of the STAT1 protein level, suggesting a decrease in the percentage of phosphorylated STAT1 molecules.
The expression profile of IRF1 in response to $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ stimulation mimicked the one of SOCS1 (Fig. 4A). In subsequent studies, we therefore focussed on SOCS1 as indicator of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ -induced gene expression.
Next, we chose a dose of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ with submaximal biological efficiency regarding inhibition of cell growth and collagen synthesis, $1\mathrm{ng/ml}$ [9]. As shown in Fig. 4B in the context of model-based computational simulations, the lower dose of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ was also able to induce and maintain STAT1 protein expression over the entire time of stimulation (although $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ at $100\mathrm{ng/ml}$ was more effective). In contrast, phospho-STAT1 as well as SOCS1 mRNA levels, after an initial increase, dropped down again and remained lower than in cells stimulated with $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ at $100\mathrm{ng/ml}$ .
## 3.2. Establishment of the reaction network
Based on the experimental data, we first established a reduced reaction network of the $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ -stimulated STAT1 signalling pathway (Fig. 3). The reaction network includes the binding of the cytokine to the $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ receptor (Ir), leading to receptor activation. The active complex is denoted by IIr. Unphosphorylated STAT1 (STAT1U) is phosphorylated by IIr. Phosphorylated STAT1 (STAT1P; not shown in Fig. 3) rapidly forms homodimers (STAT1D), which translocate into the nucleus and induce the transcription of specific target genes. When STAT1D is not bound to the DNA, dimers may dissociate, followed by protein dephosphorylation and nuclear export of the resulting STAT1U [25]. The network considers IRF1 [22,26] and SOCS1 as target genes of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ -activated signalling through STAT1, with SOCS1 as a potential negative feedback regulator inhibiting the catalytic activity of JAK1/2 [27,28]. Furthermore, it takes into account that STAT1 expression itself increases in response to $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ stimulation.
The annotation time delay in Fig. 3 refers to temporal differences between $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ action at the receptor level (including STAT1 activation) and consecutive steps, such as expression of target genes and feedback inhibition by SOCS1: First, we have observed that STAT1 tyrosine phosphorylation is followed by a delayed expression of SOCS1 and IRF1 (Fig. 4A). In accordance with previous studies [29], the STAT1 protein also displayed a delayed increase after $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ stimulation. Since the underlying regulatory mechanism is unknown so far, we have assumed it is induced by a delayed signal from the active receptor. Secondly, in our time-course studies (with $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ at $100~\mathrm{{ng/}}$ ml), STAT1P levels did not decrease despite induction of SOCS1 expression (Fig. 4A). This observation suggests that negative feedback induced by SOCS1 could be effective at late times $:\geq200\mathrm{min}$ ) only, where it could reduce the slope of the late increase of STAT1P.
In the network it is also included that STAT1U can shuttle into the nucleus, where it induces the transcription of target genes [30].
## 3.3. Mathematical model
The reaction network was translated into a system of ordinary differential equations (ODE), which describe temporal changes of the network components as a function of interactions and transport processes. Variables of the mathematical model are summarised in Table 1, the ODE model is presented in Table 2, and the respective rate equations are shown in Table 3. The variables of the model representing cellular components (left column of Table 1) are concentrations but their units are arbitrary due to the lack of standard curves. Other variables and parameters of the model are summarised in the right column of Table 1.
The kinetic model is based on the following assumptions and simplifications: (a) Only free $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ can be degraded; (b) $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ reversibly binds to Ir. IIr phosphorylates STAT1U. In the absence of experimental data, we have simplified the steps of receptor complex activation and STAT1U phosphorylation; (c) The formation of STAT1D is a rapid and high affinity process. Therefore, we have applied a quasi-steady state approximation, implying that monomers can be neglected [31,32]. In accordance with a previous model [32], the concentration of STAT1D is presented in monomer units; (d) To keep the model simple, nucleo-cytoplasmic shuttling is modelled as a free diffusion process, though the nuclear import of STAT1D is a facilitated transport [33]; (e) The quantification of the confocal microscopy data led to the result that the cytoplasmic area occupied by STAT1 is three times larger than the nuclear area (data not shown). The factor 3 is therefore included in the mathematical model at the respective positions to take into account different compartment sizes; (f) It has been suggested that nuclear export of STAT1U is controlled by tyrosine dephosphorylation of unbound STAT1 [34]. Our model therefore includes a reversible binding of STAT1D to DNA, which determines the resting of STAT1D in the nucleus, instead of considering a delay for the resting [35]; (g) Feedback inhibition of JAK1/2 by SOCS1 may reduce STAT1U phosphorylation both directly and indirectly (by blocking JAK-mediated receptor phosphorylation as a prerequisite for STAT1U binding). We have formulated this process in the mathematical model by neglecting the binding of STAT1U to the receptor; (h) The delayed processes of increased STAT1U expression, SOCS1/IRF1 transcription and negative feedback by SOCS1 are described by a distributed time delay with mean delay times $\overline{{\tau}}_{i\cdot}$ For the probability density of the delay time $\tau$ we have chosen the kernel of the Gamma function, which allows the transformation of delay differential equations into a set of ordinary differential equations [36]. The kernel function is defined in the last row of Table 3.
The first two algebraic equations in the right column of Table 2 contain relations between variables representing cellular components. The next four algebraic equations in the right column of Table 2 relate the model variables to the experimental data. Immunoblot and real-time PCR time series data can be scaled by arbitrary factors. We have chosen different scaling factors for STAT1P and mRNA expression data in the algebraic equations, $\mathsf{W B}_{\mathrm{scale}}$ and $\mathrm{PCR}_{\mathrm{scale}}$ respectively. These algebraic equations hold for the quantities of the respective cellular components.
In addition, the ratio of nuclear versus cytoplasmic concentration of STAT1 (RSNC) has been calculated from the confocal microscopy data.
Initial conditions of variables which need not to be scaled have not been optimised, and their values are summarised in Table 2 (lower part of right column). The initial condition of a variable refers to the value of the variable at the time point zero and is annotated by (0) after its name.
## 3.4. Parameter estimation
The parameters $k_{i},\overline{{\overline{{\mathbf{\psi}}}}}_{i}$ and $I_{\mathrm{tot}}$ are global parameters of the model. Local parameters, which can have different values for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{IFN}}\gamma=100\ n g/$ ml and $\mathrm{IFN}\gamma=1~\mathrm{ng/ml}$ , include the scaling factors and the initial conditions for STAT1D and SOCS1/IRF1 mRNA. Using the $\mathbf{fSSmb}$ algorithm [37] implemented in the SBtoolbox2 [38], all parameters were estimated on the basis of time series for STAT1, STAT1P and SOCS1/IRF1 mRNA by global optimisation. The results of parameter optimisation are presented in Table 4.
## 3.5. Results modelling
Fig. 4A shows a comparison between the experimental time series and the simulation results of the mathematical model (using optimised parameters) for the initial condition $\mathrm{IFN}\gamma(0)=100\mathrm{ng/ml}.$ Specifically, STAT1 protein expression, STAT1P levels, nuclear translocation of STAT1 and expression of SOCS1/IRF1 mRNA were analysed. Besides the fact that computer simulations closely matched the experimental observations, three results were considered as particularly important: First, the simulations successfully reproduced the three-phase increase pattern of
STAT1P in response to $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ stimulation. Secondly, experimental and simulation data concordantly show a delayed increase of SOCS1 and IRF1 mRNA levels compared to phosphorylation of STAT1. Thirdly, both experimental and computational results are compatible with the hypothesis that an increase of the STAT1 protein level at late times $(>200\mathrm{min})$ of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ stimulation is responsible for the late, second increase of STAT1P (at an $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ concentration of $100\mathrm{ng/ml}\cdot$ ). As shown in Fig. 4B, a high agreement between experimental and modelling results was also achieved when the $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ concentration was reduced from $1\mathrm{FN}\upgamma(0)=100$ to $1\mathrm{ng/ml}$ .
## 3.6. Model validation
To validate the mathematical model, we tested its ability to predict the results of experiments not previously used for model calibration, and analysed the dose-dependency of $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma$ signalling through STAT1. The model successfully predicted the profiles of the dose response curves of STAT1, STAT1P and SOCS1 mRNA for three different time points of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ stimulation, as shown in Fig. 5 by the close overlap of computational and experimental results.
In subsequent studies, we tested the influence of the stimulation scenario on the network dynamics by comparing the efficiency of a single dose of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ $\mathrm{{(1ng/ml)}}$ with the one of multiple smaller doses $(4\times0.25\mathrm{ng/ml}$ ; applied at intervals of $3\mathrm{h}$ [Fig. 6]). The mathematical model was able to predict the temporal profiles of total STAT1, STAT1P and SOCS1 mRNA for both stimulation modes. Even the complex staircase structure in the experimental time series of STAT1P (splitdose mode) was correctly indicated by the mathematical model. Furthermore, in case of STAT1P and SOCS1, predicted and measured slopes of increases were similar for most time points. However, for yet unknown reason the measured increase of STAT1 protein levels strongly exceeded the predicted ones. Computational and experimental studies together revealed that STAT1P and SOCS1 levels displayed a delayed maximum, when the $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ dose of $1~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}$ was split into four portions.
## 3.7. Conclusions
Based on quantitative experimental data, we have developed a mathematical model of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ signalling through STAT1 in PSC. This focus was chosen based on previous studies indicating an essential role of STAT1 in the mediation of antifibrotic effects of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ , which are of potential clinical interest in the context of chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer [9,10]. The mathematical model successfully reproduced key laboratory findings and was able to predict principle results of independent experiments. One of them compared the efficiency of different stimulation modes with respect to the expression/activation of different components of the STAT1 pathway. We considered this experimental design interesting for the following reason: Interferons are cytokines with a variety of clinical applications. Since interferon action also involves induction of negative feedback loops, the application mode (e.g., time between two applications; dose split versus a single high dose) is likely to influence biological efficiency. Thus, studies at the molecular level may provide deeper insights into the cellular basis of interferon responsiveness or resistance. In this scenario, the long-term vision is that mathematical models of interferon-induced signalling cascades may gain practical relevance with respect to the optimisation of treatment protocols.
Already at the current stage, mathematical modelling provided an added value to the experimental studies. Most of all, it could explain the temporal profiles of STAT1 activation and expression of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ target genes (SOCS1 and STAT1 itself) by interactions between key components of the STAT1 pathway and nucleo-cytoplasmic shuttling of STAT1.
Both experimental and computational results revealed that STAT1P levels did not decline over the entire course of treatment $(12\mathrm{h})$ when the cells were stimulated with a high dose of $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ $\mathrm{(100ng/ml)}$ ), although the negative feedback inhibitor SOCS1 was efficiently induced. Instead, STAT1P levels at late time points correlated with increased protein levels of the transcription factor. If the cytokine concentration was reduced to $1\mathrm{ng/ml}$ , STAT1P levels quickly declined in the course of the experiment, while STAT1 protein levels did not. A hypothesis compatible with all these observations is that an exhaustion of (biologically active) $\mathsf{I F N}\upgamma,$ together with tyrosine phosphatase action, limits STAT1 activation by phosphorylation. In the presence of saturating amounts of the cytokine, persistent generation of receptor signals and increased levels of STAT1 protein together prevent a decline of STAT1P levels.
Neither experimental data nor mathematical modelling provided compelling evidence that SOCS1 acts as an efficient feedback inhibitor in our experimental system. According to the mathematical model, SOCS1 only reduces the slope of the STAT1P increase at late time points of stimulation with $100\mathrm{ng/ml\FN\gamma}$ in a manner that the increase of STAT1 protein levels exceeds the one of STAT1P.
To further improve the predictive power of the mathematical model, studies are underway to determine the doses and the time points of experimental stimulations with mathematical methods of experimental design. Based on our current experiences, we believe that guiding of experiments in a way that a maximum of information is obtained from the studies represents one of the most powerful applications of mathematical approaches in our research field at present. With respect to $\mathsf{I F N}\gamma$ signalling, we expect that further investigations into mathematical modelling of the STAT1 pathway will also provide improved mechanistic explanations of the antifibrotic and antiproliferative interferon action.
